1. Introduction
The use of pit-latrines (PL) is the most common and
simplest form of excreta disposal, widely used by many residents
in Tanzania both in urban and rural areas. Since one
of their basic structural components is a pit in the ground,
in high water table (HWT) areas they are often completely
filled with underground water and therefore inconvenience
the users and pollute the groundwater (GW). People solve
this problem by using elevated pit-latrines (EPL). This is
basically increasing the height of the substructure above
ground level. Nonetheless, this practice solves only the
problem of convenience but does not take away the problem
of seeping dirty water, which ends up polluting the
GW. So, in order to avoid GW pollution, the pit has to
be completely sealed, a situation that makes it a genuine
accumulation (AC) system as liquid and solids both accumulate
over a period of time. The aims of accumulation
systems are to (1) end up with stable sludge at the time
when the reactor is full with faecal sludge (Xdeg = 0 g
COD/l); (2) have zero VFA at the time of emptying the
reactor contents; and (3) reuse the produced biogas.
Accumulation systems for liquid and solid manure have
been investigated under thermophilic, mesophilic and psychrophilic
conditions by Zeeman (1991) and El-Mashad
(2003); co-digestion of concentrated black water and
kitchen refuse in an accumulation system within a decentralised
sanitation and reuse (DESAR) concept was studied
by Kujawa-Roeleveld et al. (2002). The black water studied
by Kujawa-Roeleveld et al. (2002) was collected with the
use of a vacuum toilet as collection and transport system,
implying that electricity is an important component for
the system to function. This is not a suitable system for
